# Deploying to a VPS

Real, HTTPS deployment of the full stack (Postgres + backend + dashboard)
behind [Caddy](https://caddyserver.com/) for automatic Let's Encrypt TLS on
one domain, no subdomains needed. Works on any VPS with Docker installed —
DigitalOcean, Hetzner, Linode, AWS Lightsail, etc. This is a separate,
production-shaped compose file (`docker-compose.prod.yml`) from the
local-dev one (`docker-compose.yml`) — see `README.md`'s "Deploying
(Docker)" section for that one.

Verified locally end-to-end (real HTTPS termination, real path-based
routing to the backend, real dashboard serving including a client-side-only
route) before this doc was written — see `PROGRESS.md` for the details.
The one thing that genuinely can't be verified without a real domain is the
actual Let's Encrypt certificate issuance (ACME needs a real, publicly
resolvable domain and port 80 reachable from the internet) — that step is
the same well-established Caddy behavior used by a huge number of
production deployments, not something new invented for this project.

## 1. Provision a VPS

Any provider, any size — this is a small internal tool, a $5-6/mo VPS
(1 vCPU, 1-2GB RAM) is plenty to start. Requirements:

- Docker + the Docker Compose plugin installed (most providers offer a
  "Docker" marketplace image that has this preinstalled; otherwise follow
  [Docker's install docs](https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/) for
  whatever Linux distro you picked).
- Ports 80 and 443 open to the internet (check the provider's firewall
  panel, not just the OS firewall).

## 2. Point a domain at it

Add an `A` record (and `AAAA` if the VPS has IPv6) for your domain pointing
at the VPS's public IP. Wait for it to propagate (`dig yourdomain.com`
should return the VPS's IP) before continuing — Caddy's certificate request
in step 4 will fail if the domain doesn't resolve yet.

You do NOT need separate subdomains for the backend and dashboard — one
domain serves both. `https://yourdomain.com/api/*` routes to the backend,
everything else routes to the dashboard (see `Caddyfile`).

## 3. Copy the repo and configure

```bash
git clone https://github.com/Taha-Mahmoodi/atlas-fieldforce.git
cd atlas-fieldforce
cp deploy.env.example .env
```

Edit `.env` and fill in every value — `deploy.env.example` documents each
one, including a copy-pasteable command to generate a real VAPID keypair in
the exact format `pywebpush` expects (not a PEM — verified against
`pywebpush`'s actual key-parsing code before writing that command into the
file, not guessed).

## 4. Start the stack

```bash
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml up -d --build
```

First boot: Caddy requests a Let's Encrypt certificate for your domain
(needs port 80 reachable, per step 1), the backend runs
`alembic upgrade head` automatically (see `backend/docker-entrypoint.sh`),
then everything starts. Watch it happen:

```bash
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml logs -f
```

Once it settles, `https://yourdomain.com` serves the dashboard and
`https://yourdomain.com/api/v1/...` serves the backend, both over a real,
auto-renewing TLS certificate — Caddy renews it automatically well before
expiry, nothing to schedule yourself.

## 5. Verify

```bash
curl https://yourdomain.com/api/v1/auth/register \
  -X POST -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -H "X-Tenant-Id: $(uuidgen)" \
  -d '{"email":"you@example.com","password":"a-real-password","role":"manager"}'
```

Expect a real `access_token`/`refresh_token` JSON response. Then open
`https://yourdomain.com` in a browser and log in with the tenant ID printed
above (the `X-Tenant-Id` you sent) plus the email/password.

## Updating to a new version

```bash
git pull
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml up -d --build
```

Migrations run automatically on every restart via the entrypoint script —
there's no separate migration step to remember.

## Building the tablet app against this deployment

Set `VITE_API_BASE_URL=https://yourdomain.com` in `tablet/.env` before
running `tablet/README.md`'s documented build sequence — the app needs to
point at your real, reachable backend to be useful on a physical device
(pointing it at `localhost` would only work on the machine that built it,
not on a device you install it to).

## What this doesn't do

- **No backup strategy** for the `fieldforce_pg_data` volume — set up your
  own Postgres backup approach (a cron'd `pg_dump` to object storage is the
  usual minimum) before trusting this with real data.
- **No zero-downtime deploys** — `docker compose up -d --build` restarts
  the backend container, which drops in-flight requests for a few seconds.
  Fine for an internal tool at this scale; revisit if that ever matters.
- **Runs as root, single process** — the backend container has no
  non-root user or worker-process management configured. Acceptable for a
  small internal deployment; a larger-scale production hardening pass
  would add both.
